From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emuTOS-MCD <darekadumon@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 10:12:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 148/190] D: could never be re-mounted, and that is the whole
 symptom

"Impossible to open D: on boot, no matter how many retries; works after
the diagnostic's third round." Not the wake, not the dead-drive latch,
not the decoder. The mount.

blkdev_getbpb() runs once, during the boot probe. If the disc is not
readable at that moment -- and it is not, because nothing has woken the
drive that early -- bdev->bpb.recsiz stays zero, and blkdev_rwabs()
answers ESECNF on sight for every logical access afterwards:

    if (blkdev[dev].bpb.recsiz == 0)
        return ESECNF;

Nothing below that line runs again. The drive can wake, the decoder can
lock, the disc can be perfectly readable, and D: still refuses -- and
refuses identically however many times the critical-error alert is
answered with Retry, because the retry re-enters at the same test.
Getbpb is only re-run when a media change is reported, and
segacd_cd_ioctl() returned MEDIANOCHANGE unconditionally on the
grounds that the tray cannot be opened with the power on. True, and
beside the point: "the medium changed" is also how a driver asks for a
volume to be logged again.

CDTEST calls bios_getbpb(3) as the first thing in every round. That is
the one call in the system that re-attempts the mount, and it is why
round 3 "unlocks" the drive.

So: until one block has come off this disc, report MEDIACHANGE. After
that the answer is no forever, which is true and self-limiting;
disk_mediach() will not ask more than twice a second in any case.

Two others on the same failure path:

The cool-down bought an attempt and then crippled it. cd_deadrun was
left one short of the threshold, so the attempt ran on the short leash
cd_read_block reserves for a drive that has just failed, got one try
instead of two, and re-latched the moment it did not work. Ten seconds
of patience spent on an attempt weaker than a normal one. It gets a
whole fresh run now; three more failures still latch it.

And the bring-up wake goes back to the loader's twenty seconds. Cutting
it to 300 frames was justified with "a visit costs about 174 ticks",
which came from the gap between two diagnostic rounds only one of which
ran DRV_INIT. That was not a measurement of DRV_INIT and should not
have been used as one.
---
 bios/scdapi.h |  2 +-
 bios/segacd.c | 64 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++------
 2 files changed, 58 insertions(+), 8 deletions(-)

diff --git a/bios/scdapi.h b/bios/scdapi.h
index 5ed00e1..6916684 100644
--- a/bios/scdapi.h
+++ b/bios/scdapi.h
@@ -28,7 +28,7 @@
 #define SCDAPI_H
 
 #define SCD_COOKIE   0x53674344L        /* 'SgCD' */
-#define SCD_VERSION  16
+#define SCD_VERSION  17
 
 /* Everything the disc diagnostic prints, in one go. Taken in a single
  * call rather than twenty accessors so that the numbers in one report
diff --git a/bios/segacd.c b/bios/segacd.c
index 61a1021..911bcaa 100644
--- a/bios/segacd.c
+++ b/bios/segacd.c
@@ -3068,9 +3068,17 @@ static BOOL cd_read_block(ULONG block, UBYTE *buf)
              * Put back afterwards, because a read that genuinely needs
              * the drive spun up from cold is a different question from
              * a boot probe. */
-            segacd_bios_initwait = 300UL;
+            /* The loader's own budget for this call, and no less.
+             *
+             * A previous version cut it to 300 frames on the grounds
+             * that a firmware visit costs about 174 of the diagnostic's
+             * ticks. That number came from the gap between two rounds,
+             * only one of which ran DRV_INIT, so it was not a
+             * measurement of DRV_INIT at all. boot/m1emu.S allows
+             * twenty seconds for exactly this call and the drive
+             * answers inside it on every boot; there is no honest
+             * reading that says less will do. */
             cd_woke = (UBYTE)(segacd_bios_read(0UL, 0) == 0 ? 1 : 2);
-            segacd_bios_initwait = 1200UL;
         }
     }
 
@@ -3253,9 +3261,18 @@ LONG segacd_cd_rw(WORD rw, LONG sector, WORD count, UBYTE *buf)
     if (cd_dead) {
         if (hz_200 - cd_deadstamp < CD_DEAD_COOLDOWN)
             return EREADF;      /* answer immediately, as it has been */
-        cd_dead = 0;            /* one more try, and one failure from
-                                 * being right back where it was */
-        cd_deadrun = CD_DEADRUN - 1;
+        cd_dead = 0;
+        /* A whole fresh run, not one crippled try.
+         *
+         * This used to leave cd_deadrun one short of the threshold, so
+         * the attempt that the cool-down had just bought was taken on
+         * the short leash cd_read_block reserves for a drive that has
+         * just failed, with one try instead of two, and re-latched the
+         * moment it did not work. Ten seconds of patience spent on an
+         * attempt deliberately weaker than a normal one. If the drive
+         * is genuinely dead it takes three more failures to say so
+         * again, which is what the threshold is for. */
+        cd_deadrun = 0;
     }
 
     while (count--) {
@@ -3769,8 +3786,41 @@ LONG segacd_cd_ioctl(WORD op, void *arg)
         return E_OK;
     case GET_MEDIACHANGE:
         /* The tray cannot be opened without cutting power to the sub,
-         * which ends EmuTOS along with the question. */
-        return MEDIANOCHANGE;
+         * so this never reports a real disc swap. What it does report
+         * is a volume that has never been logged, and that is not the
+         * same question.
+         *
+         * blkdev_getbpb() runs once, during the boot probe. If the disc
+         * is not readable at that moment -- and on this machine it is
+         * not, because nothing has woken the drive yet -- then
+         * bdev->bpb.recsiz stays zero, and blkdev_rwabs() answers
+         * ESECNF on sight for every logical access afterwards:
+         *
+         *   if (blkdev[dev].bpb.recsiz == 0)
+         *       return ESECNF;
+         *
+         * Nothing below that line ever runs again. The drive can wake
+         * up, the decoder can lock, the disc can be perfectly readable,
+         * and D: still refuses -- and refuses identically however many
+         * times the critical-error alert is answered with Retry,
+         * because the retry re-enters at the same test. Getbpb is only
+         * re-run when a media change is reported, and this returned
+         * MEDIANOCHANGE unconditionally.
+         *
+         * That is the whole of "impossible to open D: on boot, and it
+         * works after the diagnostic": CDTEST calls Getbpb directly,
+         * which is the one thing in the system that re-attempts the
+         * mount. The wake, the latch and the decoder were all innocent
+         * of this particular symptom.
+         *
+         * So: until one block has come off this disc, say the medium
+         * changed, and let GEMDOS try to log the volume again. Once a
+         * block has been delivered the answer is no forever, which is
+         * both true and self-limiting. disk_mediach() will not ask more
+         * than twice a second. */
+        if (cd_dtest & 2)
+            return MEDIANOCHANGE;
+        return MEDIACHANGE;
     }
     return ERR;
 }
